# Tesseract environment variables — copy this file to .env.local and
# fill in the values. .env.local is gitignored and stays on your machine.
#
# Production / staging values live in Netlify's Environment Variables
# dashboard, not here.

# ─── Supabase (required) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Get these from Supabase dashboard → Settings → API.
# The anon key is safe to commit in client code (it's what your browser
# uses); the service_role key is NOT — it bypasses RLS and must never
# leave Bitwarden or .env.local.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-or-publishable-key

# ─── Sentry error tracking (optional) ────────────────────────────────────
# Sentry only initializes when VITE_SENTRY_DSN is set, so local dev stays
# quiet by default. Production and staging set these in Netlify's
# Environment Variables dashboard. The DSN is safe to expose in the
# client bundle (Sentry uses domain allow-lists for security).
VITE_SENTRY_DSN=
# Logical environment name reported to Sentry — typically "production" or
# "staging". Falls back to Vite's MODE if unset.
VITE_SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=

# ─── Financial data providers ────────────────────────────────────────────
# There are no client-side provider credentials, and there must never be.
#
# This file used to ask for Alpha Vantage and Finnhub keys here. Vite inlines
# every VITE_* variable into the bundle as a string literal, so setting one
# published it: a working Alpha Vantage key was served as plain text in
# dist/assets/index-*.js to anyone who loaded the app. Removed 2026-09-10;
# nothing reads either name any more.
#
# The browser now uses only key-free sources — Yahoo Finance for quotes, with
# Finnhub's published `demo` token behind it. Provider credentials live in
# Supabase function secrets; see the market news section below.
#
# If you find a VITE_* variable elsewhere asking for a provider key, it is a
# mistake, and src/lib/security/__tests__/no-browser-provider-keys.test.ts
# fails on it — in this file as well as in source.

# ─── Financial data layer behavior ───────────────────────────────────────
# The VITE_FINANCIAL_DATA_* knobs documented here are removed. They
# configured src/lib/financial-data/client.ts, which has no importers — the
# ProviderManager half of that module is unreachable from the application, so
# the knobs tuned nothing. See the note in financial-data/config.ts.

# ─── Market news (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used by the `market-news` edge function, which merges company news from
# several providers for the Ideas feed. These are set as Supabase *function*
# secrets, not client env vars — they must never reach the browser bundle:
#
#   supabase secrets set FINNHUB_API_KEY=...
#   supabase secrets set ALPHAVANTAGE_API_KEY=...
#
# Each is independently optional. With neither set, the function still
# returns Yahoo Finance results (no key required), so the feed works and
# gets richer as keys are added. Alpha Vantage is the only one of the three
# that supplies sentiment and per-ticker relevance scores.

# ─── Where inbound access and demo requests go (both optional) ───────────
#
# The public landing page at / and the Early Access notice on /signup both
# route "Request a demo" / "Request access" through these, in this order:
#
#   1. VITE_ACCESS_REQUEST_URL   an external intake form (Typeform, Tally,
#                                a Google Form). It exposes no address to
#                                scrapers, filters spam, and returns
#                                structured answers. Opens in a new tab.
#                                Set this to the form's public URL.
#   2. Neither set               the landing-page buttons open an on-page
#                                request box that posts to Netlify Forms.
#                                This is the live route, and it needs no
#                                variable here: the form is declared in
#                                public/forms/access-request.html and
#                                detected at build time. Submissions arrive
#                                under Forms in the Netlify dashboard.
#                                The endpoint exists only on a real deploy,
#                                so a local submission always lands in the
#                                box's failure state. That is expected.
#
# VITE_ACCESS_REQUEST_EMAIL is still read, but it is NOT a mode any more. It
# is the box's fallback if the POST fails, shown only to a visitor who is
# already looking at an error, so it is never sitting on the page for a
# scraper to harvest. The /signup notice still renders it as a mailto, which
# is the one place a configured address is public.
#
# Do not put a personal address here without deciding to publish it.
VITE_ACCESS_REQUEST_URL=
VITE_ACCESS_REQUEST_EMAIL=

# ─── Legal documents: TWO gates, both OFF ────────────────────────────────
#
# Leave both unset. They are separate on purpose, because they fail
# differently and get turned on at different times. The sequence is
# docs/legal/legal-release-checklist.md.
#
# Only the exact string "true" opens either gate. Vite inlines every VITE_*
# value as a string, so "false" is itself a truthy string — the gate compares
# the literal, which is why setting one of these to "false" turns it off
# rather than on.

# Whether the legal document pages serve their text.
#
# OFF: /legal and /legal/<id> still render, showing a short "in preparation"
# notice. Nothing false is served and no footer link is dead.
#
# ON: requires `npm run legal:publish` to have run, which REFUSES while any
# document is still a draft or any field in src/legal/entity.ts is null.
# Publishing a policy that names an entity which does not exist is a false
# statement, and that is worse than a missing document.
#
# To review the drafts locally:
#   npm run legal:publish -- --preview
#   VITE_LEGAL_PUBLISHED=true
#   npm run legal:unpublish        # when you are done
VITE_LEGAL_PUBLISHED=

# Whether a new account must affirmatively accept the terms, and whether
# that writes a record to public.terms_acceptances.
#
# OFF today, and it stays off even when set, until BOTH acceptance-required
# documents are status 'approved' AND publication is on. Those two extra
# conditions live in src/legal/publication.ts rather than in a checklist:
#
#   * accepting a DRAFT would manufacture a record of consent to a document
#     no lawyer has read, which is worse than no record because it looks
#     like consent;
#   * accepting a document the user cannot OPEN is unenforceable in most
#     jurisdictions as well as unfair.
#
# Requires supabase/migrations/20261125120000_terms_acceptances.sql, which is
# NOT applied. See docs/legal/legal-release-checklist.md stage 2.
VITE_LEGAL_ACCEPTANCE_ENABLED=
